hard.why:
If you decide *not* to take a value, you must not take any later copy of it either; otherwise "skip the first 2, take the second" rebuilds the subset "take the first 2, skip the second". So the skip branch jumps to the first index with a different value:

```java
int j = i + 1;
while (j < a.length && a[j] == a[i]) j++;
subs(j, a, cur, res);
```

For `{1, 2, 2}` that yields 6 subsets: `[1,2,2] [1,2] [1] [2,2] [2] []`. Plain `i + 1` produces `[1,2]` and `[2]` twice. Skipping on the pick side means the second 2 can never be picked, so `[2,2]` and `[1,2,2]` are lost.

**Takeaway:** in pick / not-pick, "not pick" for a value means "take this value zero more times".
